        "id": 1423861
      },
      "writtenOn": "2019-04-11T17:50:08Z",
      "side": 1,
      "message": "If we want to follow a functional programming style, I think it makes the most sense conceptually to instantiate and initialize the mock in one place (this also lets us get rid of the setup() method altogether). Since Kotlin lets us do this (while Java doesn\u0027t), I think we should take advantage of this.\n\n(This is all under the assumption that the mocks are reset between individual test methods i.e. I understood this sentence correctly: \"I was under the false impression that the same instance of the test class was reused, but after checking that\u0027s not the case.\")",
      "parentUuid": "1c380f53_3fcd1977",
